1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ahmad Sanjar (Mu'iz ud-Dīn Ahmad-e Sanjar (Turkish: Sultan Sancar also Ahmed Sindjar)) (1085 – May 8, 1157) was the sultan of the Great Seljuq Empire from 1118 to 1153. He was initially the sultan of Khorasan until
2 CONC  he gained the rest of the territory upon the death of Muhammad I.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a son of Malik Shah I and participated in wars of succession against his three brothers and an uncle, namely Mahmud I, Barkiyaruq, Malik Shah II and Muhammad I. In 1096, he was given the province of Khorasan to govern under
2 CONC  his brother Muhammad I.[1] Over the next several years Ahmed Sanjar became the ruler of most of Persia with his capital at Nishapur. A number of rulers revolted against him and continued the split of the Great Seljuk Empire that 
2 CONC the dynastic wars had started. In 1102, he repulsed an invasion from Kashgaria, killing Jibrail Arslan Khan near Termez.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sanjar undertook a campaign to eliminate the Assassins of Alamut, and successfully drove them from a number of their strongholds.[2] However, an anecdote indicates that en route to their stronghold at Alamut, Sanjar woke up one da
2 CONC y to find a dagger beside him, pinning a note from Hassan Sabbah stating that he (Hassan) would like peace. Sanjar, shocked by this event, sent envoys to Hassan and they both agreed to stay out of each other's way.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1141, Sanjar marched to confront the Kara Khitan threat and engaged them near Samarkand at the Battle of Qatwan. He suffered an astounding defeat — his first — and escaped with only fifteen of his elite horsemen, losing all Sel
2 CONC juk territory east of the Syr Darya (Jaxartes).[4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Oghuz Turks from Khuttal and Tukharistan captured Ahmed Sanjar in 1153 and held him until 1156.[6] He died the following year and was buried at Merv, where his tomb was destroyed by the Mongols during their invasion of the Khwarez
2 CONC mian Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmed Sanjar married Turkan Khatun (d. 1156) and he had two daughters with her - wives of his nephew Mahmud.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her death Ahemed Sanjar married Rusudan, daughter of Demetre I of Georgia, widow of sultan Masud Temirek. He had no children with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ a b Grousset, René (1970) The Empire of the Steppes Rutgers University Press, New Brunswick, New Jersey, USA, p. 159, ISBN 0-8135-0627-1
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Franzius, Enno (1969) History of the Order of Assassins Funk and Wagnalls, New York, p. 59, OCLC 23676
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Lewis, Bernard (1968) The Assassins: A Radical Sect in Islam Basic Books, New York, p. 30, OCLC 436364
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Ibn al-Athir as cited by Zarncke, Friedrich (1879) Der Priester Johannes S. Heizel, Leipzig, p. 856-857 OCLC 7619779
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Liao Shih (the official history of the Khitan Dynasty) cited by Wittfogel, Karl A. and Feng Chia-Sheng (1949) History of Chinese Society: Liao, 907-1125 American Philosophical Society, Philadelphia, p. 639 OCLC 9811810
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Sinor, Denis (1990) The Cambridge History of Early Inner Asia Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, England, p. 368, ISBN 0-521-24304-1
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1150, Ahmad Sanjar, the last of the great Seljuq monarchs, created a province out of these lands and called it Kurdistan. The province of Kurdistan, formed by Sanjar, had as its capital the village Bahar (which means lake o
2 CONC r sea), near ancient Ecbatana (Hamadan). It included the vilayets of Sinjar and Shahrazur to the west of the Zagros mountain range and those of Hamadan, Dinawar and Kermanshah to the east of this range. An autochthonous civilizati
2 CONC on developed around the town of Dinawar (today ruined), located 75 km North-East of Kermanshah, whose radiance was later only partially replaced by that of Senna, 90 km further North.[26] ref: https://www.wikiwand.com/en/History_o
2 CONC f_the_Kurds
