1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaykhusraw_III '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kaykhusraw III''' (Old Anatolian Turkish: كَیخُسرو, Persian: غياث الدين كيخسرو بن قلج ارسلان‎, Ghiyāth ad-Dīn Kaykhusraw bin Qilij Arslān) was between two and six years old when in 1265 he was named Seljuq Sultan of Rûm. He was
2 CONC  the son of Kilij Arslan IV, the weak representative of the Seljuq line who was controlled by the Pervane, Mu’in al-Din Suleyman.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reign=
2 CONT
2 CONT Mu’in al-Din Suleyman, empowered by the Mongol khan Abagha, had Kilij Arslan IV executed in 1266. The young Kaykhusraw became no more than a figurehead and played no part in the events of his reign, which were dominated first by t
2 CONC he Pervane and later by the Mongol vizier of Rum, Fakhr al-Din Ali.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1283 Kaykhusraw was co-opted by the Mongol Kangirtay into a revolt against the Ilkhan sovereign Ahmad. Kaykhusraw was executed for his involvement in the rebellion in March of 1284.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaykhusraw III was the last Seljuq sultan buried in the dynastic mausoleum at the Alaeddin Camii in Konya.
2 CONT
2 CONT The throne of Kaykhusraw III, a fine example of Seljuq woodcarving, survives in the Ethnography Museum of Ankara. The throne was previously housed in the Kızıl Bey Camii in Ankara.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
