1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sargis II Jaqeli''' (Georgian: სარგის II ჯაყელი) (died 1334) was a Georgian prince (''mtavari'') and ruler of Principality of Samtskhe during 1306-1334.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Prince Beka I Jaqeli. During the reign of his father, he made advances against the Turks and their invasions. Sargis II was made Amirspasalari by King George V of Georgia "the Brilliant".
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References===
2 CONT
2 CONT # ქართული საბჭოთა ენციკლოპედია, ტომი, 9, გვერდი, 102, თბილისი, 1985
2 CONT
2 CONT ==============
2 CONT In 1334, King George V endowed his maternal uncle, Sargis II of the Jakeli clan, with the title of atabeg, which his descendants wore until the 17th century. Accordingly, their principality Samtskhe became known as “atabegsky” ( S
2 CONC amtskhe-Saatabago ). Atabeg separatist aspirations played a significant role in the collapse of the centralized Georgian state in 1490.
2 CONT
2 CONT ref: https://www.wikiwand.com/ru/%D0%94%D0%B6%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B8
