1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''NOTE: On the English Wikipedia, he is descried as the nephew of Kaykaus II, but in all the other languages, he is Kaykaus II's grandson.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kayqubad III''' (Old Anatolian Turkish: كَیقُباد, Persian: علاء الدین کیقباد بن فرامرز‎ ʿAlāʾ ad-Dīn Kayqubād bin Farāmurz) was briefly sultan of the Sultanate of Rum between the years of 1298 and 1302. He was a nephew of the d
2 CONC eposed Kaykaus II and had strong support among the Turkmen. As sultan he was a vassal of the Mongols and exercised no real power.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reign=
2 CONT
2 CONT He first appears circa 1283 as a pretender to the Seljuq throne. He was recognized by the Turkmen Karamanids, but he was defeated by vizier Fakhr al-Din Ali and Kaykhusraw III and sought refuge in Cilician Armenia.[1] Nothing is k
2 CONC nown of his movements again until 1298, when he was appointed to the sultanate by the Ilkhan Mahmud Ghazan upon the downfall of Masud II. He purged the Seljuq administration of his predecessor’s men with extreme violence and becam
2 CONC e deeply unpopular; as a result when he visited the Ilkhan in 1302, he was executed and replaced with his predecessor Mesud II in order to keep the peace.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
