1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Beyhan Sultan''' (Ottoman Turkish: بیحان سلطان; "leader of Kings", ante 1494 - 1559), also known as Peykhan Sultan, was an Ottoman princess, daughter of Selim I and Hafsa Sultan. She was the sister of Sultan Sul
2 CONC eiman the Magnificent.[1][2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyhan_Sultan_(daughter_of_Selim_I)_ En]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyhan_Sultan_(daughter_of_Selim_I)#Marriage '''Marriage''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Beyhan married Ferhad Pasha[3] in 1513.[4] Ferhad, initially served as the third vizier, and contributed essential military expertise to Suleiman during the early years of his reign. However, a turning point emerged upon Suleiman'
2 CONC s return from Rhodes in 1523, triggering accusations against Ferhad, ranging from misappropriation of property to mismanagement of the sultan's troops. Rather than opting for execution, Suleiman, potentially influenced by interces
2 CONC sion from Hafsa and Beyhan, chose to demote Ferhad to the distant district governorship of Semendire. This geographical separation aimed to mitigate perceived threats to Constantinople, but Ferhad's fortunes further soured when hi
2 CONC s forces suffered a defeat by the Hungarians near Semendire in August 1523, leading to his dismissal from all positions.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite these setbacks, Hafsa and Beyhan's persistent intercession secured an audience for Ferhad with Suleiman in November 1524, in Edirne. Unfortunately, Ferhad's efforts to convince Suleiman of his innocence proved futile. Frus
2 CONC tration boiled over during the meeting, with Ferhad vehemently declaring himself a victim of a high-level conspiracy. Suleiman, compelled to act, ordered Ferhad's removal. Ferhad's continued protests, combined with the brandishing
2 CONC  of a dagger, escalated the situation, resulting in his imprisonment and eventual execution by decapitation. Suleiman, reportedly intending only imprisonment, found himself forced into a more severe response due to Ferhad's violen
2 CONC t behavior. The aftermath of Ferhad's execution left Beyhan inconsolable, severing ties with Suleiman as she chose seclusion, marking the beginning of a permanent estrangement between them.[1] Refusing to remarry, she lived in sel
2 CONC f-exile from Constantinople.[5], but according to some sources she was later forced by Suleiman to remarry Mehmed Pasha.[6] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyhan_Sultan_(daughter_of_Selim_I)#Death_and_burial '''Death and burial]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Beyhan Sultan died in her palace a Skopje in 1559.[7][8] Her resting place is located inside the türbe of her father Selim I in Yavuz Selim Mosque.[7] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyhan_Sultan_(daughter_of_Selim_I)#Issue '''Issue''']
2 CONT
2 CONT By Ferhad, Beyhan had at least twi children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Esmehan Hanımsultan.[9] She married Koca Sinan Pasha and had a son, Mehmed Pasha, and three daughters, Emine Hanım, Hatice Hanım and Hüma Hanım. Widowed in 1596, she remarried with Güzelce Mahmud Pasha.[10][5]
2 CONT * At least another child. Documents relating to Ferhad Pasha's execution mention his and Beyhan's children when they talk about his family's grief, but without specifying their name, number or gender.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyhan_Sultan_(daughter_of_Selim_I)#References References]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyhan_Sultan_(daughter_of_Selim_I)#Sources Sources]
2 CONT *  Öztuna, Yılmaz (2006). Kanuni Sultan Süleyman (in Turkish). Babıali Kültür Yayıncılığı. ISBN 978-975-8486-99-1.
