1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paolo_Renier
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Paolo Renier''' (born Venice, November 21, 1710 – died there February 13, 1789) was a Venetian statesman, the 119th, and penultimate, Doge of Venice. He was considered a good orator and tactician, and served as ambassador to Co
2 CONC nstantinople and to Vienna. His election as Doge was unpopular, and he was the subject of numerous menacing letters at the time. Renier was succeeded as Doge by Ludovico Manin, who would be the last Doge of Venice. He married Gius
2 CONC tina Dona (d. 1751) in 1733, and Margherita Delmaz (d. 1817) in 1751. Paolo Renier ( Venice , November 21 1710 - Venice , February 13 1789 ) was the centodiciannovesimo doge of the Republic of Venice , from 14 January 1779 until h
2 CONC is death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Andrew and Elizabeth Morosini during his staves, the penultimate story of the lagoon, there occurred important facts, even if abroad set the stage for the outbreak of the French Revolution , that a few years after the death
2 CONC  of Renier sweep away the 'ancient Republic of Venice.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT * https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/paolo-renier_(Dizionario-Biografico)/
2 CONT
2 CONT RENIER, Paolo. - He was born in Venice in the family palace, in S. Stae, on 21 November 1710 (State Archives of Venice, Avogaria di Comun, reg. 63 (= XIII), c. 316v) by Andrea di Daniele and Elisabetta Morosini di Girolamo, fifth 
2 CONC of eight brothers and had as godfather at the spring Almorò Grimani di Marino, wise of the mainland (2 December 1710, Venice, Historical Archives of the Patriarchate, S. Stae Church, Baptisms, reg. 1, c. 312r).
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniele and Paolo died at an early age, there were: Girolamo, administrator in the Mint, mayor of Brescia and councilor of Venice for the district of S. Croce; Antonio, sopracomito, captain in the Gulf, general administrator in Da
2 CONC lmatia, general administrator of Mar, censor, captain in Padua, senator; Alvise, councilor of Comun and councilor of Venice for the district of S. Croce; Daniele, wise to the Orders and chamberlain of Comun, soon ended his career 
2 CONC for having married the not noble Agnese Rota (2 June 1758); the youngest son, Angelo Maria, became a senator.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had an accurate education from the abbot Pietro Antonio Muazzo (Del Negro, 1986, p. 249): deep knowledge of the classics - some sources assert that he knew by heart, in Greek, the Iliad and the Odyssey - and an uncommon mastery
2 CONC  rhetoric, placed at the service of an innate predisposition to politics. These qualities, and fate - he extracted the Balla d’oro on 4 December 1730, entering the Maggior Consiglio before he came of age - destined him to a rapid 
2 CONC career. He married Giustina Donà di Leonardo on 28 April 1733 (State Archives of Venice, Avogaria di Comun, reg. 94 (= VII), c. 213v), mother of his two children, Andrea, ambassador to Rome between 1775 and 1777 , who married Ceci
2 CONC lia Manin on 2 September 1754, and Leonardo. Giustina Donà was the bearer of a probably substantial dowry: four fidecommessi of the Bergonzi and Tasca families - each of which had bought the nobility in the urgencies of the war of
2 CONC  Candia for 100,000 ducats -, although from the 'liquidity' difficult to determine (28 February 1733, ibid., Notarial, Acts, reg. 2123, cc. 271r-275r).
2 CONT
2 CONT After his apprenticeship in the saviato to the Orders (28 June 1736 and 16 March 1737), he gained experience as an executor at the Waters (12 May 1737). He entered the College, first as a wise man of the mainland, elected every ye
2 CONC ar from November 23, 1737 to December 31, 1750, almost always with a delegation to the office of cashier, and then, from December 9, 1751 to December 30, 1761 he was wise of the Council eleven times . His constant presence in the 
2 CONC College and in the Senate - where, having entered on March 27, 1746, he remained, without interruption, from 1750 to 1764 - soon made him the point of reference of the reformist faction of the Venetian aristocracy, alongside perso
2 CONC nalities such as the Memmo brothers, Andrea and Bernardo di Pietro, and Angelo Querini di Lauro, from the S. Severo branch. The crisis that sanctioned the end of the Patriarchate of Aquileia (9 March 1748 - 6 July 1751) saw him, a
2 CONC llying his part with the influential procurator of S. Marco di Ultra Giovanni Emo di Pietro, among the most intransigent in the negotiations with the S Seat: that is, he wanted not the suppression, as it later happened, but the tr
2 CONC ansfer of the Patriarchate to Udine. He therefore came to support, and impose, the extreme measure of the withdrawal of the ambassador to Rome, Pietro Andrea Cappello di Pietro Girolamo (July 4, 1750). He was still a protagonist b
2 CONC y supporting the strategies of the wise man of Mainland Sebastiano Foscarini di Alvise. He proposed in the Senate a decree (September 7, 1754) by which the Venetian subjects were required to submit to the College any act involving
2 CONC  the court of Rome (Gullino, 1971, p. 68). The decree was withdrawn, but the heated debate had now made him the leader of his faction.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since then, he had prestigious positions, all of them senatorial: supreme administrator of the new Justice (8 July 1750), savio alle Acque (8 January 1753, 22 January 1757 and 4 January 1758), executor over the Blasphemy (18 Janua
2 CONC ry 1755 February 8, 1759), one of the eight compliment ambassadors for the election of Pope Clement XIII, deputy to the Provision of money (January 19, 1759), administrator over the monasteries (January 10, 1761). With Andrea Tron
2 CONC  di Nicolò, in September 1760, he succeeded in having an army reform approved by the Scottish general William Graham, then in command of the land forces of the Serenissima (Del Negro, 1998, p. 58). Elected supreme administrator of
2 CONC  Health (January 14, 1761), his appeal to Angelo Querini, avogadore di Comun in office, for a trivial quarrel between secular religious and brotherhoods for the 'provisions' due at the funeral, led to an institutional clash over p
2 CONC owers, considered excessive, by the state inquisitors (Venice, Marciana National Library, Mss. It., cl. VII, 776 (= 7285): P. Franceschi, Istoria della correction ..., c. 5v). Querini exiled to Verona, Renier was the referent of t
2 CONC he party called Querini. Although he was not among the five correctors of the Laws delegated to trat
